G514.d:
ἄξιος, -α, -ον
 (ἄγω, in sense, to weigh), [in LXX for בֵּן (Deu.25:2), מָלֵא, שָׁוָה; frequently in Wis, 2Mac ;] 
  1. Part One 
  (a) of weight, worth (often with genitive, cf. Pr 315, 811), before πρός: Rom.8:18 (see Field, Notes, 157); 
  (b) befitting, meet: with genitive, Matt.3:8, Luke.3:8 23:41, Acts.26:20, 1 Cor.16:4 (see M, Pr., 216); absol., 2 Thess.1:3; 
  (with) of persons, worthy; 
  2. Part Two
  α in good sense: with genitive of thing(s), Matt.10:10, Luke.7:4 10:7, Acts.13:46, 1 Tim.1:15 4:9 5:18 6:1; with aor. inf. (see M, Pr., 203): Luke.15:19, 21, Act_13:25, Rev.4:11 5:2, 4 5:9, 12; before ἵνα: John.1:27; ἅς, Luke.7:4; absol., but of what understood: Matt.10:11, 13 22:8, Rev.3:4; with genitive of person(s), Matt.10:37-38, Heb.11:38; 
  β in bad sense; with genitive of thing(s), Luke.12:48 23:15, Acts.23:29 25:11, 25 26:31, Rom.1:32; absol., Rev.16:6 (MM, VGT, see word).†
 (AS)

G575.d:
ἀπό (on the frequently neglect of elision bef. vowels, see Tdf., Pr., 94, WH, App., 146), 
 prep. with genitive (WM, 462ff.; on its relation to ἐκ, παρά, ὑπό, ib. 456f.), [in LXX for לְ ,בְּ ,מִן ;]  
from (i.e. from the exterior). 
  1. Of separation and cessation; 
  (1) of motion from a place: Matt.5:29, 30 7:23, Luke.5:2  22:41, al.; 
  (2) in partitive sense (M, Pr., 72, 102, 245; MM, see word; Bl., §40, 2), Matt.9:16  27:21, John.21:10, Acts.5:2, al.; also after verbs of eating, etc.; 
  (3) of alienation (cl. genitive of separation), after such verbs as λούω (Deiss., BS, 227), λύω, σώζω, παύω, etc.; ἀνάθεμα ἀ., Rom.9:3; ἀποθνήσκειν ἀ., Col.2:20; σαλευθῆναι, 2 Thess.2:2, καθαρός, -ίζειν, ἀ. (Deiss., BS, 196, 216), Acts.20:26, 2 Cor.7:1, Heb.9:14; 
  (4) of position, Matt.23:34 24:31, al.; after μακράν, Matt.8:30; transposed before measures of distance, John.10:18 21:8, Rev.14:20 (Abbott, JG, 227); 
  (5) of time, ἀπὸ τ. ὥρας, ἡμέρας, etc., Matt.9:22, John.19:27, Acts.20:18, Phil.1:5, al.; ἀπ᾽ αἰῶνος, Luke.1:70, al.; ἀπ᾽ ἀρχῆς, etc., Matt.19:4, Rom.1:20; ἀπὸ βρέφους, 2 Tim.3:15; ἀφ᾽ ἧς, since, Luke.7:45, al.; ἀπὸ τ. νῦν, Luke.1:48, al.; ἀπὸ τότε, Matt.4:17, al.; ἀπὸ πέρυσι, a year ago, 2 Cor.8:10 9:2; ἀπὸ πρωΐ, Acts.28:23; 
  (6) of order or rank, ἀπὸ διετοῦς, Matt.2:16; ἀπὸ Ἀβραάμ, Matt.1:17; ἐβδομος ἀπὸ Ἀδάμ, Ju 14; ἀπὸ μικροῦ ἕως μεγάλου, Acts.8:10, Heb.8:11; ἄρχεσθαι ἀπό, Matt.20:8, John.8:9, Acts.8:35, al. 
  2. Of origin; 
  (1) of birth, extraction, and hence, in late writers, 
  (a) of local extraction (cl. ἐξ; Abbott, JG, 227ff.), Matt.21:11, Mark.15:43, John.1:45, Acts.10:38, al.; οἱ ἀπὸ Ἰταλίας (WM, §66, 6; M, Pr., 237; Westc, Rendall, in l.), Heb.13:24; 
  (b) of membership in a community or society (BL, §40, 2), Acts.12:1, al.; 
  (with) of material (= cl. genitive; Bl. l.with; M, Pr., 102), Matt.3:4 27:21; 
  (d) after verbs of asking, seeking, etc., Luke.11:50, 51 1 Thess.2:6 (Milligan, in l.); 
  (2) of the cause, instrument, means or occasion (frequently = ὑπό, παρά, and after verbs of learning, hearing, knowing, etc.; Bl., §40, 3), Matt.7:16 11:29, Luke.22:45, Acts.2:22 4:36 9:13 12:14, 1 Cor.11:23, Gal.3:2, al.; ἀπὸ τ. ὄχλου, Luke.19:3 (cf. John.21:6, Acts.22:11); ἀπὸ τ. φόβου, Matt.14:26, al. (cf. Matt.10:26 13:44). 
  3. Noteworthy Hellenistic phrases: φοβεῖσθαι ἀπό (M, Pr., 102, 107); προσέχειν ἀπό (M, Pr., 11. with; Milligan, NTD, 50); ἀπὸ νότου (Heb. מִגֶּנֶב), Rev.21:13; ἀπὸ προσώπου (מִפְּנֵי), 2 Thess.1:9 (Bl., §40, 9); ἀπὸ τ. καρδιῶν (בְּלֵב), Matt.18:35; ἀπὸ ὁ ὤν (WM, §10, 2; M, Pr., 9), Rev.1:4. 
  4. In composition, ἀπό denotes separation, departure, origin, etc. (ἀπολύω, ἀπέρχομαι, ἀπογράφω); it also has a perfective force (M, Pr., 112, 247), as in ἀφικνεῖσθαι, which see
 (AS)

G622.d:
ἀπ-όλλυμι and ἀπολλύω 
 [in LXX for אָבַד, etc. (38 words in all)]. 
  1. Act., 
  1 to destroy utterly, destroy, kill: Mk 124, 922, al.; τ. ψνχήν, Matt.10:28, al.; 
  2 to lose utterly: Matt.10:42, al.; metaphorically, of failing to save, John.6:39 18:9. 
  2. Mid., 
  1 to perish; 
  (a) of things: Matt.5:29, John.6:12, Heb.1:11 (LXX, al.; 
  (b) of persons: Matt.8:26, al. Metaphorical, of loss of eternal life, John.3:15-16 10:28 17:12, Rom.2:12, 1 Cor.8:11 15:18, 2 Pet.3:9. In οἱ ἀπολλὺμενοι, the perishing, contrasted in 1 Cor.1:18, al., with οἱ σωζόμενοι, the "perfective" force of the verb, WH. " implies the completion of the process of destruction," is illustrated (see M, Pr., 114 f.; M, Th., ii, 2:10); 
  2 to be lost: Luke.15:4 21:18. Metaphorical, on the basis of the relation between shepherd and flock, of spiritual destitution and alienation from God: Matt.10:6 15:24, Luke.19:10 (MM, see word; DCG, i, 191 f., ii, 76, 554; Cremer, 451) 
 (AS)

G649.d:
ἀπο-στέλλω, 
 [in LXX very freq., almost always for שׁלח ;] 
prop., to send away, to dispatch on service; 
  1. to send with a commission, or on service; 
  (a) of persons: Christ, Matt.10:40; the apostles, 10:16; servants, Mark.12:2; angels, 13:27; 
  (b) of things: ὄνος, Matt.21:3; τὸ δρέπανον, Mark.4:29; τ. λόγον, Acts.10:36; τ. ἐπαγγελίαν (i.e. the promised Holy Spirit), Luke.24:49, Rec.; before εἰς, Matt.20:2, Luke.11:49, John.3:17; ὀπίσω, Luke.19:14; ἔμπροσθεν, John.3:28; πρὸ προσώπου, Matt.11:10; πρός, Matt.21:34; with ref. to sender or place of departure: ἀπό, Luke.1:26 (Rec. ὑπό); παρά, John.1:6; ἐκ, ib. 1:19; ὑπό, Acts.10:17 (Rec. ἀπό); before inf., Mark.3:14, al.; ἵνα, Mark.12:2, al.; εἰς (of purpose), Heb.1:14; without direct obj.: before πρός, John.5:33; λέγων, John.11:3; ἀποστείλας, with indic., Matt.2:16, Acts.7:14, Rev.1:1. 
  2. to send away, dismiss: Luke.4:18, Mark.5:10 8:26 12:3 (cf. ἐξ-, συν-αποστέλλω). 
 SYN.: πέμπω, the general term. ἀ. "suggests official or authoritative sending" (see Thayer, see word πέμπω; Westc., Jo., 298; Epp. Jo., 125; Cremer, 529; MM, see word). (AS)

G703.d:
ἀρετή, -ῆς, ἡ 
 [in LXX, in sing.: Hab.3:3, Zec.6:13 (הוֹד), in pl.: Isa.42:8, 12 43:21 63:7 (תְּהִלָּה), Wis.4:1 5:13 8:7 2:1-24; 4Mac.2.2 * ;] 
prop., whatever procures pre-eminent estimation for a person or thing, in Hom. any kind of conspicuous advantage. Later confined by philos. writers to intrinsic eminence-moral goodness, virtue; 
  (a) of God: 2 Pet.1:3; 
  (b) of men: Phil.4:8, 2 Pet.1:5; pl. (Isa, Es, ll. with), excellencies: 1 Pet.2:9 (the usage appears to be a survival of an early comprehensive sense in which the original idea is blended with the impression which it makes on others, i.e. praise, renown; see Hort, 1 Pe., 129. Deiss., BS, 95 f., thinks it means manifestations of divine power, as in current Gk. speech; cf. also MM, see word)†
 (AS)

G862.d:
ἄ-φθαρτος, -ον
 (φθείρω), [in LXX: Wis.12:1 18:4 * ;] 
imperishable, immortal; 
  (a) of things: 1 Cor.9:25, 1 Pet.1:4 1:23 3:4; 
  (b) of persons: of men, 1 Cor.15:52; of God, Rom.1:23, 1 Tim.1:17 (MM, see word).†
 (AS)

G891.d:
ἄχρι
 (and Epic ἄχρις, bef. vowel (see MM, see word), Rom.11:25 T, Gal.3:19 T, WH, mg., Heb.3:13); 
  1. adv., utterly (Hom.). 
  2. Prep. with genitive, until, unto, as far as; 
  (a) of time Acts.3:21 22:22, Rom.1:13 5:13, 1 Cor.4:11, 2 Cor.3:14, Gal.4:2, Phil.1:6; ἄ. καιροῦ, Luke.4:13, Acts.13:11; ἄ. ἧς ἡμέρας, Matt.24:38, Luke.1:20 17:27, Acts.1:2 (τῆς ἡ. ἧς), Acts.1:22; ἄ. ταύτης τ. ἡμέρας (τ. ἡ. ταύτης), Acts.2:29 23:1 26:22; ἄ. ἡμερων πέντε, Acts.20:6; ἄ. αὐγῆς, Acts.20:11; ἄ. τοῦ νῦν, Rom.8:22, Phil.1:5; ἄ. τέλους, Heb.6:11, Rev.2:26; 
  (b) of space: Acts.11:5 13:6 20:4 (R, txt., WH, mg.) Acts.28:15, 2 Cor.10:13-14, Heb.4:12, Rev.14:20 18:5; 
  (with) of measure or degree: ἄ. θανάτου, Acts.22:4, Rev.2:10 12:11. 
  3. As conj., until; 
  (a) ἄχρι alone: with subj. aor., Gal.3:19 (ἄ. οὗ T, WH, mg.); id. without ἄν (Bl., § 65, 10), Rev.7:3 15:8 20:3, 5; with indic. fut., Rev.17:17; 
  (b) ἄ. οὗ (i.e. ἄ.. τούτου ᾧ): with indic. aor., Acts.7:18; impf., Acts.27:33; with subj. aor. (Bl., ut supr.), Luke.21:24, Rom.11:25, 1 Cor.11:26 15:25, Gal.3:19, T, WH, mg.; id. with ἄν, Rev.2:25; with indic. pres., while, Heb.3:13 (cf. μέχρι).†
 (AS)

G903.d:
Βαλαάμ, ὁ, indecl. (Heb. בִּלְעָם), as in LXX (FlJ has ὁ βάλαμος); 
 Balaam (Num.22-24): 2 Pet.2:15, Ju 11, Rev.2:14.†
 (AS)

G907.d:
βαπτίζω 
 (βάπτω), [in LXX: 4Ki.5:14 (טבל), Isa.21:4, Jdth.12:7, Sir.31 (34)30 * ;] 
to dip, immerse, sink; 
  1. generally (in Polyb., iii, 72, of soldiers wading breast-deep; in i, 51, of the sinking of ships); metaphorically, to overwhelm (Is, l.with; cf. MM, Exp., x); with cogn. accusative, βάπτισμα β., Mark.10:38, 39 Luke.12:50. Mid., 
  2. to perform ablutions, wash oneself, bathe (Ki, Jth, Si, ll. with): Mark.7:4; aor. pass, in same sense, Luke.11:38. 
  3 Of ablution, immersion, as a religious rite, to baptize; 
  (a) (a) absol.; Mark.1:4, John.1:25, 26 28 3:22, 23, 26 4:2 10:40, 1 Cor.1:17; ὁ βαπτίζων (= ὁ βαπτιστής, M, Pr., 127), Mark.6:14, 24; with accusative, John.4:1, Acts.8:38, I Co.1.14, 16; with cogn. accusative, τὸ βάπτισμα, Acts.19:4 (cf. Mark.10:38, supr.); pass., to be baptized, receive baptism: Matt.3:13, 14, 16, Mark.16:16, Luke.3:7, 12 21 7:29 (τ. βάπτισμα) ib. 30, Acts.2:41 8:12, 13 36 9:18 10:47 16:15, 33 18:8; mid., 22:16 (M, Pr., 163); 
  (b) with prepositions: ἐν, of the element, Matt.3:6, 11 Mark.1:4, 5 8, Luke.3:16, John.1:26, 31 33 3:23, Acts.1:5 2:38 10:48 11:16, 1 Cor.10:2; εἰς, of the element, purpose or result (Lft., Notes, 155), Matt.3:1 28:19, Mark.1:9, Acts.8:16 19:3, 5 Rom.6:3, 1 Cor.1:13, 15 10:2 12:13, Gal.3:27; with dative, ὕδατι, Luke.3:16, Acts.1:5 11:16; ὑπὲρ τῶν νεκρῶν, perh. to fulfil the wish of a dead friend, 1 Cor.15:29 (see ICC, in l.; cf. DB, i, 238ff.; DCG, i, 169a; ii, 605b; Cremer, 126).
 † (AS)

G908.d:
βάπτισμα, -τος, τό (βαπτίζω), prop., the result of the act, τὸ βαπτίζειν, as distinct from βαπτισμός, the act itself, 
 immersion, baptism; 
  1. metaphorically, of affliction: Mark.10:38, 39 Luke.12:50. 
  2. Of the religious rite of baptism; 
  (a) of John's baptism: Matt.3:7 21:25, Mark.11:30, Luke.7:29 20:4, Acts.1:22 10:37 18:25 19:3; β. μετανοίας, Mark.1:4, Luke.3:3, Acts.13:24 19:4; 
  (b) of Christian baptism; Rom.6:4, Eph.4:5, Col.2:12 (Tr., -μῷ, which see), 1 Pet.3:21 (cf. Cremer, 130; Tr., Syn. §xcix).
 † (AS)

G922.d:
βάρος, -εος, τό, 
 [in LXX: Jdg.18:21 (כּבד), Jdth.7:4, Sir.13:2, 2Ma.9:10, 3Ma.5:47 * ;] 
weight; 
  (a) a weight, burden, lit. and metaphorically: Matt.20:12, Acts.15:28, 2 Cor.4:17, Rev.2:24; ἀλλήλων τὰ β., one another's faults, Gal.6:2; ἐν β., burdensome: 1 Thess.2:6, R, txt., but see infr.; 
  (b) in late Gk. (Soph., Lex., see word), dignity, authority: ἐν βάρει, 1 Thess.2:6 (R, mg.; see Milligan, ICG, in l.).† 
 SYN.: ὄγκος, an encumbrance; φορτίον, a burden, that which is borne. (AS)

G945.d:
βατταλογέω, -ῶ (Rec. βαττολ-, D, βλαττ- = βατταρίζω, prob. onomatop.; see MM, see word; DCG, ii, 499b, 790a); 
 to stammer, repeat idly: Matt.6:7 (Cremer, 765).†
 (AS)

G991.d:
βλέπω, 
 [in LXX chiefly for ראה, also for פּנה, etc. ;] 
  1. of bodily sight; 
  (a) to see, have sight (opposite to τυφλὸς): Matt.12:22, John.9:7, Acts.9:9, Rom.11:8, Rev.3:18, al.; 
  (b) to perceive, look (at), see: absol.,  Acts.1:9; with accusative, Matt.7:3, Mark.5:31, Luke.6:41, John.1:29, al.; ὅραμα, Acts.12:9; γυναῖκα, Matt.5:28; βιβλίον, Rev.5:3, 4; τ. βλεπόμενα, 2 Cor.4:18. 
  2. Metaphorical, of mental vision; 
  (a) to see, perceive, discern: absol., Matt.13:13, Luke.8:10; δι ̓ ἐσόπτρου, 1 Cor.13:12; with accusative, Heb.2:9 10:25; before ὅτι, Heb.3:19, Jas.2:22; 
  (b) to consider, look to, take heed: absol., Mark.13:23, 33; with accusative, 1 Cor.1:26, al.; before πῶς, with indic., Luke.8:18, 1 Cor.3:10, Eph.5:15; before τί, with indic., Mark.4:24; before εἰς πρόσωπον, of partiality, Matt.22:16, Mark.12:14. Colloq. (for ex. from π., V. Deiss., LAE, 122; M, Pr., 107; MM, Exp., x; Milligan, NTD, 50), β. ἑαυτόν: Mark.13:9; before ἵνα μή, II Jo 8; β. ἀπό, Mark.8:15 12:38; before μή (cl. ὁρᾶν), with fut. indic., Col.2:8, Heb.3:12; id. with aor. subj., Matt.24:4, Mark.13:5. 
  3. Of situation and direction (Lat. specto), to look, face (towards), places, etc. (before πρός, Xen, Hell., vii, 1, 17; Eze.40:23, 24): before κατά, with accusative, Acts.27:12 (cf. ἀνα-, ἀπο-, δια-, ἐμ-, ἐπι-, περ-, προ-βλέπω), see DCG, i, 446; ii, 596.
 (AS)
